          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I am 34 weeks pregnant with swelling in my hands and a headache that will not shift. How urgent is this, and what should I be asking about?",
          "answer": "**Please contact your maternity team or a doctor today.** Do not wait for a routine appointment. The guidance on dangerous headaches lists a new headache in pregnancy as needing same-day medical assessment, and it names swelling as one of the features to take especially seriously (*Sudden severe headache and other dangerous headache patterns*, emergency medicine and neurology society guidance). Your symptoms match that description, so I am treating this as same-day, even though the automated check labelled it routine. The check's label does not outweigh that passage.\n\n**Call emergency services now** if you have any of these: a severe headache that is getting worse, changes to your vision, pain in the upper abdomen, confusion, new weakness or numbness, difficulty speaking, a seizure, or trouble breathing. The guidance lists visual changes and upper abdominal pain as features that go with headache in pregnancy. Also seek immediate care if the headache suddenly becomes the worst you have ever had.\n\n**Questions to ask when you call or are seen:**\n- Do I need to be examined today, and where should I go?\n- Should my blood pressure and urine be checked now?\n- Which warning signs mean I should go straight to emergency care rather than phone first?\n- What should I do if the headache eases but the swelling stays or spreads?\n\nThese sources do not tell me what is causing your symptoms, and I cannot say whether anything serious is happening. Only a clinician who examines you can do that.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
